# Target B — five distinct persistent bars flatten by exact arithmetic

**Date:** 2026-09-22  
**Starting SHA:** `84d937ca93decb710b829fc610d46e0d89684988`  
**Current base:** `43601c43`; state `2026-09-14.audit.51`  
**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; artifact unreviewed.  
**Level I / Target B:** unchanged, open.

## 1. Statement and exact consequence

Use the pure strict-rhombic disk, backward kernel `K`, persistent equality set
`J`, and rotation functionals `s_h` of the
[tangent-capacity candidate](target_b_tangent_cubic_capacity_2026-09-21.md).
Its canonical geometric inputs are Target B §§40–43. Assume a gradient balance
`mu` is strictly positive on **every** persistent bar.

**Theorem F5.** If `|J|<=5` and the rotation functionals of the persistent bars
are pairwise distinct on `K`, every persistent diameter bar is exactly constant
on a neighborhood of zero in the train-angle slice `theta(0)+K`.

If `K!=0` and the source is diameter-pair-minimal among triangle-free
configurations of that cardinality and diameter, it has no active diameter bars
outside `J`. This does not exclude a same-diameter flat family. Selection is
conditional on the selected source retaining the pure-disk contact complex.

The implication chain is

```text
integer phase counts + exact source-gradient balance
 -> one of six five-vertex transfer supports
 -> rational ratios among three specific stress weights
 -> forbidden rational point on one of two rank-zero elliptic curves
 -> zero phase matrix -> exact simultaneous flattening.
```

The new result closes the five-distinct-bar higher-order case, not Target B.
Repeated rotation classes, unsupported persistent bars, larger supports,
full backward rank, same-diameter families, and topology corrections remain.

## 2. Source-to-finite-universe reduction

From AP/PG in the companion, define integer off-diagonal counts `N_ij` and
`B_ij=N_ij`, `B_ii=-sum_{j!=i}N_ij`. Then

\[
B\mathbf1=0,\qquad Bs=0,\qquad \mu^T B=0.
\tag{F1}
\]

Every nonzero `N_ij` is realized by a tangent train. Distinct rotation
functionals imply that this train separates only bars `i,j` within `J`.
Consequently

\[
\mu_i<\mu_j,\qquad
|v_i\cdot v_j|=|v_i|^2,
\qquad v_i=\mu_i e_i.
\tag{F2}
\]

Order the bars by nondecreasing weight. The support of nonzero `N_ij` is a
nonempty forward directed graph if `B!=0`. Ties cause no loss: no edge can
join equal weights, and any total tie-breaking is permitted. With five labels,
there are exactly `2^10` subsets of the ten forward edges; no additional
symmetry quotient is taken. Smaller supports are included by isolated vertices.

The following necessary tests eliminate supports:

1. No vertex has outdegree one: its row of `Bs=0` would equate two distinct
   functionals.
2. A source with nonzero outdegree has outdegree at least three. Its column of
   `mu^T B=0` makes its diagonal zero; two outgoing signed counts would then
   be opposite, and `Bs=0` would equate their two destinations.
3. A sink cannot have indegree one, by its column of `mu^T B=0`.
4. Two vertices have at most two common higher recipients. The two absolute
   projection equations in F2 intersect in at most four vectors, or two
   unoriented directions, because distinct diameter directions are not parallel.
5. If `i->j`, they have at most one common higher recipient. The FF proof in
   the companion solves this explicitly: in coordinates `v_i=(a,0)`,
   `v_j=(a,b)`, a common higher recipient is, up to sign, the unique vector
   `(a,-b-2a^2/b)` distinct from `v_j`.

The deterministic verifier exhausts all 1024 subsets and leaves exactly the
following six supports. An edge `ij` means `i->j`.

| Case | Edges | Geometric type |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A1 | 12,13,15,23,24,34,35 | triangle 123 |
| B1 | 12,14,15,23,24,34,35 | chain 123, common higher 4 |
| A2 | 12,13,14,23,25,34,35 | triangle 123 |
| B2 | 12,14,15,23,25,34,35 | chain 123, common higher 5 |
| C1 | 12,13,14,24,25,34,35 | fork 1 to 2,3, common higher 4 |
| C2 | 12,13,15,24,25,34,35 | fork 1 to 2,3, common higher 5 |

This table is a proved finite lemma via the complete explicit enumeration;
it does not assume that any surviving support has a geometric realization.

## 3. The three source weights are commensurate

In every row of outdegree two, the diagonal of `B` is nonzero: if the two
outgoing counts summed to zero, `Bs=0` would equate the destinations.

Column 2 has only the nonzero off-diagonal entry from row 1 and the nonzero
diagonal from row 2. Thus `mu_1/mu_2` is rational. In cases B1/B2, column 3
similarly forces `mu_2/mu_3` rational. In C1/C2 it forces `mu_1/mu_3` rational.
In A1, the sink column 4 has precisely the two nonzero entries from rows 2,3;
in A2, sink column 5 does. Hence `mu_2/mu_3` is rational in those cases too.
All three weights are therefore rational multiples of one another. Only
**integer** source counts justify this step; arbitrary real signed flows do not.

## 4. Three geometric obstructions

Two elementary arithmetic facts will be certified in §5:

\[
\begin{split}
&(x^2-1)(y^2-1)=4
 &&\text{has no rational solution with }x,y>1;\tag{R1}\\
&z^2=x^4+6x^2-3
 &&\text{has no rational solution with }x>1.\tag{R2}
\end{split}
\]

### Triangle type A

Orient `v_1=(a,0)`, `v_2=(a,b)` with `a>0`, `b!=0`. The common higher
recipient `v_3` is `(a,-b-2a^2/b)` up to sign. Thus

\[
\left(\left({\mu_2\over\mu_1}\right)^2-1\right)
\left(\left({\mu_3\over\mu_2}\right)^2-1\right)=4.
\]

Both ratios are rational and exceed one, contradicting R1.

### Common-higher fork type C

Orient each of `v_1,v_2,v_3` to have positive dot product with their common
higher recipient `w`. For any edge `i->j` within these three vertices, the dot
product `v_i dot v_j` is **negative**. Otherwise the two projection equations
would force `w=v_j`, impossible for a higher recipient.

Write `v_1=(a,0)`, `v_2=(-a,b)`, and `v_3=(-a,c)`. The equations with `w`
give `w_x=a` and

\[
w_y=b+2a^2/b=c+2a^2/c.
\]

The two diameter directions are distinct, so `b!=c`, and hence `bc=2a^2`.
It follows that

\[
\left(\left({\mu_2\over\mu_1}\right)^2-1\right)
\left(\left({\mu_3\over\mu_1}\right)^2-1\right)=4,
\]

again contradicting R1.

### Common-higher chain type B

Put the common higher vector at `w=(R,0)`, and orient the three lower vectors
to have positive dot product with it. They lie on its diameter circle and have
the form

\[
v_i={R\over1+t_i^2}(1,t_i),\qquad
\mu_i={R\over\sqrt{1+t_i^2}}.
\]

The negative-dot observation gives, for the two chain edges,

\[
t_1=-t_2-2/t_2,\qquad t_2=-t_3-2/t_3.
\]

None of these denominators is zero: zero would identify a lower vector with
the higher recipient. Set `r=mu_2/mu_1>1` and `s=mu_3/mu_2>1`, both rational.
Direct substitution yields

\[
r^2=1+{4(s^2-1)\over(s^2+1)^2},\qquad
[r(s^2+1)]^2=s^4+6s^2-3.
\]

This contradicts R2. All six supports are therefore excluded; `B=0`, and
groupwise cancellation in the exact train formula proves F5. ∎

## 5. Complete arithmetic certificates for R1 and R2

Only standard rational 2-isogeny descent and good-reduction torsion injection
are imported. Their hypotheses and normalization were checked in the author's
copies of [Dixit–Murty–Pathak, §2](https://www.imsc.res.in/~anupdixit/rankproblem.pdf)
and [Stoll, Theorem 19.5 and §22](https://www.mathe2.uni-bayreuth.de/stoll/teaching/ElliptischeKurven-SS2023/Notes-EllipticCurves-pub-screen.pdf).
No database rank, BSD assumption, or unproved equality classification is used.

For `E: Y^2=X^3+aX^2+bX`, with `b(a^2-4b)!=0`, its 2-isogenous curve has
coefficients `a'=-2a`, `b'=a^2-4b`. The descent map sends a nonzero
`X`-coordinate to its rational squareclass, sends `(0,0)` to `b`, and the
identity to 1. Its image is supported on signed squarefree divisors of `b`.
A class `d` requires a primitive integral solution of

\[
N^2=dM^4+aM^2e^2+(b/d)e^4.
\tag{R3}
\]

The rank satisfies `2^(rank+2)=|im alpha_E| |im alpha_E'|`.

### R1: the curve `Y^2=X(X-1)(X+3)`

Here `(a,b)=(2,-3)`, and the isogenous coefficients are `(-4,16)`.
The first descent image consists of all four classes `1,-1,3,-3`, represented
by the identity and the points with `X=-1,3,-3`. On the isogenous curve,
`Y^2=X((X-2)^2+12)` forces `X>=0`, so only classes 1 and 2 remain.
Class 2 would require

\[
N^2=2M^4-4M^2e^2+8e^4.
\]

For primitive `(M,e)`, its residue modulo 16 is respectively 2, 6, or 8 when
`(M,e)` has parity `(odd,even)`, `(odd,odd)`, or `(even,odd)`. None is a square.
Thus the second image has size one and the rank is zero.

The discriminant is 2304, so 5 is a good prime. Direct enumeration gives
`#E(F_5)=8`; good-reduction injection bounds rational torsion by eight.
The eight exhibited rational points are

\[
O,\ (0,0),\ (1,0),\ (-3,0),\ (-1,\pm2),\ (3,\pm6).
\]

They are therefore all rational points. A putative R1 solution maps to
`X=x^2>1`, `Y=xy(x^2-1)`. The only possible coordinate above one is `X=3`,
which is not a rational square. This proves R1.

### R2: the curve `Y^2=X^3+6X^2-3X`

Start with `E_0: Y^2=X^3-3X^2+3X=(X-1)^3+1`, with `(a,b)=(-3,3)`.
Its descent image is `{1,3}`: the cubic has the sign of `X`, and `(3,3)`
represents class 3. Its 2-isogenous curve is `E_2: Y^2=X^3+6X^2-3X`.
Its possible classes are `1,-1,3,-3`, and it contains `1,-3`. Class 3 requires

\[
N^2=3M^4+6M^2e^2-e^4.
\]

If `3` does not divide `e`, the residue modulo 3 is 2. If `3` divides `e`,
primitivity gives `3` not dividing `M`, and the residue modulo 9 is 3.
Both are impossible for a square. Since the descent image is a group and
contains `-3`, class `-1` is excluded too. The two image sizes are two and two;
both isogenous curves have rank zero.

The discriminant of `E_2` is 6912, and `#E_2(F_5)=6`. Its complete rational
point set is therefore

\[
O,\ (0,0),\ (1,\pm2),\ (-3,\pm6).
\]

A putative R2 solution maps to `X=x^2>1`, `Y=xz`, contradicting this list.

## 6. Reproduction and scope audit

Run

```text
python3 research/verify_target_b_five_bar_arithmetic_2026_09_22.py
python3 tools/check_theorem_state.py
```

The verifier exhausts all 1024 labeled forward support graphs, checks the exact
six-member cover and every rational-weight propagation witness, certifies the
mod-16 and mod-9 exclusions, counts both good reductions, verifies the complete
listed rational point sets against their equations, and checks the geometric
and quartic substitutions symbolically. No floating-point search is a premise.

The standard descent and torsion-injection theorems, not finite point searches,
justify completeness of the rational-point lists. The graph enumeration is
exhaustive only because §2 maps every nonzero source phase matrix into its finite
universe. No source points, contacts, inactive inequalities, or unsupported
persistent bars are silently removed. The full-support and distinct-functional
hypotheses remain essential. All new claims are **proved candidates awaiting
promotion**; no canonical state or route status is changed.